/**
\addtogroup optimizers_quasi_newton

This page documents two quasi-Newton methods, BFGS and L-BFGS, in namespace `laplace::optimizers` (headers `<laplace/optimizers/bfgs.hpp>` and `lbfgs.hpp`). They are header-only templates that run on the host; nothing is marked `LAPLACE_HD` and no library is linked. A run is `minimize(bfgs_optimizer<>{}, f, x0)`, with the objectives, stopping rules, line searches and report of \ref optimizers "the optimizers page". On the standard test problems both methods converge with \f$f - f^* \le 6.3 \times 10^{-8}\f$, except L-BFGS on Wood's function, where it stops near a saddle point.

## Quasi-Newton methods

Both methods minimize an objective modeling \ref laplace::optimizers::gradient_objective "gradient_objective" with a symmetric positive definite approximation \f$B\f$ of the inverse Hessian, built from the accepted steps and the changes of the gradient.

The class template \ref laplace::optimizers::bfgs_optimizer "bfgs_optimizer" (`bfgs_optimizer<Rule, LineSearch>`) keeps a dense \f$n \times n\f$ inverse-Hessian approximation \f$B\f$, requiring \f$O(n^2)\f$ memory and work per iteration, where \f$n\f$ is the parameter dimension. After accepting a step \f$s = x_{+} - x\f$ from the current point \f$x\f$ to \f$x_{+}\f$ with gradient change \f$y = g_{+} - g\f$ from \f$g = \nabla f(x)\f$ to \f$g_{+} = \nabla f(x_{+})\f$, it updates \f$B\f$ by the BFGS formula (Nocedal and Wright, 2006, Algorithm 6.1):
\f[ B_{+} = (I - \rho s y^\top) B (I - \rho y s^\top) + \rho s s^\top, \qquad \rho = 1 / (s^\top y), \f]
with \f$I\f$ the identity.

The class template \ref laplace::optimizers::lbfgs_optimizer "lbfgs_optimizer" (`lbfgs_optimizer<Rule, LineSearch>`) never forms \f$B\f$. It retains the last `memory` curvature pairs \f$(s, y)\f$, with default \f$m = 10\f$, taking \f$O(m n)\f$ memory and work per iteration. It computes \f$B g\f$ by the two-loop recursion started from \f$\gamma I\f$, where \f$\gamma = (s^\top y) / (y^\top y)\f$ uses the most recent accepted pair (Nocedal and Wright, 2006, Algorithms 7.4 and 7.5). The first pass runs over the stored pairs from newest to oldest and removes from a copy of \f$g\f$ its components along the \f$y\f$; after the scaling by \f$\gamma\f$, the second pass runs from oldest to newest and adds back components along the \f$s\f$.

Both optimizers take a stopping rule `Rule` and a line search `LineSearch` as template parameters, defaulting to \ref laplace::optimizers::decrement_stop_rule "decrement_stop_rule" and \ref laplace::optimizers::wolfe_line_search "wolfe_line_search" (Nocedal and Wright, 2006, Algorithms 3.5 and 3.6). At compile time, \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_rule_for "stop_rule_for" checks `Rule` against \ref laplace::optimizers::quasi_newton_provides "quasi_newton_provides", which combines `stop_needs_gradient | stop_needs_decrement | stop_needs_previous`. Any stopping rule requiring at most the gradient, the decrement, and the previous point and value is accepted. The \ref laplace::optimizers::armijo_line_search "armijo_line_search" (Dennis and Schnabel, 1996) is also accepted; because Armijo evaluates only objective values during backtracking, the optimizer evaluates the gradient once more at the accepted point.

Each iteration takes the direction \f$d = -B g\f$, searches along it from step \f$1\f$, and updates the model with the accepted pair \f$(s, y)\f$ using the initial identity scaling of Shanno and Phua (1978) when the curvature condition holds. The initial steepest-descent scaling, curvature-pair skip rule, and model resets are detailed in the entries of \ref laplace::optimizers::bfgs_optimizer "bfgs_optimizer" and \ref laplace::optimizers::lbfgs_optimizer "lbfgs_optimizer".

## Stationarity and confirmation

The quasi-Newton methods report the stationarity decrement defined in the "Report and status" section of \ref optimizers "the optimizers page", extended with a curvature floor and a one-step confirmation. Before asking the stopping rule at every iteration, including iteration \f$0\f$ at the start, the optimizer computes the next direction \f$d\f$ and sets `decrement` to
\f[ \frac{1}{2} \max\bigl(g^\top B g,\ \gamma_{\max}\, g^\top g\bigr), \f]
where \f$g^\top B g = |g^\top d|\f$ for the next direction \f$d\f$, and \f$\gamma_{\max}\f$ is the largest \f$(s^\top y) / (y^\top y)\f$ over the accepted pairs (\f$0\f$ before any pair). Because \f$B\f$ is accurate only along the directions its pairs have sampled and keeps the scale of the stiff directions elsewhere, \f$\frac{1}{2} g^\top B g\f$ alone can lie many orders of magnitude below \f$f - f^*\f$ on an ill-conditioned problem, where \f$f^*\f$ is the minimum value. The floor \f$\gamma_{\max} g^\top g\f$ gives every direction at least the largest inverse curvature seen.

A convergence verdict of the stopping rule (`converged` or `converged_at_resolution`) is confirmed on the next iteration: the rule must hold twice in a row. If the confirming line search finds no acceptable step, with outcome `step_below_rounding`, `below_resolution`, `not_descent_direction` or `evaluation_budget`, the pending verdict stands. Confirmation costs one extra iteration per converged run. A `stopped_on_change` verdict is not confirmed and terminates immediately. Starting at an exact stationary point with \f$g = 0\f$ ends with status `converged` after \f$0\f$ iterations.

## Settings and status

Both classes expose their settings as public members (`stop_rule`, `line_search`, `max_iterations`, `max_evaluations`, `curvature_tolerance`, `trace`, together with `max_skipped_updates` for BFGS and `memory` for L-BFGS). Their defaults, effects, and termination statuses (with `curvature_evaluations` always \f$0\f$) are documented under \ref laplace::optimizers::bfgs_optimizer "bfgs_optimizer" and \ref laplace::optimizers::lbfgs_optimizer "lbfgs_optimizer".

## Accuracy and cost

The figures below were measured with gcc on one thread, with the default Wolfe line search and decrement tolerance \f$10^{-6}\f$. The standard test problems were run from their usual starts, also with constants \f$1\f$, \f$10^3\f$ and \f$10^6\f$ added to \f$f\f$. Every run converges with \f$f - f^* \le 6.3 \times 10^{-8}\f$, except L-BFGS on Wood's function. The distance \f$\max_i |x_i - x^*_i|\f$ to the minimizer \f$x^*\f$ is at most \f$6.1 \times 10^{-3}\f$ on Powell singular, whose Hessian is singular at \f$x^*\f$, and at most \f$9.9 \times 10^{-5}\f$ elsewhere. Each Wolfe trial evaluates the value and the gradient.

| Problem | BFGS iterations | BFGS value evaluations | L-BFGS iterations | L-BFGS value evaluations |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Rosenbrock (\f$n = 2\f$) | 35 | 41 | 36 | 43 |
| Rosenbrock (\f$n = 100\f$) | 36 | 42 | 36 | 43 |
| Powell singular | 31 | 32 | 31 | 33 |
| Beale | 14 | 15 | 14 | 15 |
| Brown badly scaled | 12 | 24 | 13 | 25 |
| Helical valley | 29 | 31 | 26 | 29 |
| Wood | 35 | 43 | 19 | 22 (saddle) |

A run takes 11 to 36 microseconds, except Rosenbrock with \f$n = 100\f$: 356 microseconds for BFGS and 94 for L-BFGS.

On quadratics of dimension \f$n = 10\f$ with condition number \f$\kappa\f$, BFGS converges up to \f$\kappa = 10^8\f$, in 39 iterations at \f$\kappa = 10^2\f$ and 130 at \f$10^8\f$, with \f$f - f^* \le 4.7 \times 10^{-9}\f$; at \f$10^{10}\f$ and \f$10^{12}\f$ it ends `stalled`. L-BFGS with memory 10 converges up to \f$\kappa = 10^6\f$, in 30 to 249 iterations with \f$f - f^* \le 2.3 \times 10^{-7}\f$.

At \f$\kappa = 10^8\f$ and \f$\kappa = 10^{10}\f$, L-BFGS with memory \f$10\f$ is declared converged about \f$10^{-2}\f$ above \f$f^*\f$ because the remaining error lies along directions that no stored or past pair has sampled, so neither \f$B\f$ nor \f$\gamma_{\max}\f$ detects it. On Wood's function, L-BFGS stops near the saddle point at \f$f = 7.877\f$, where a positive definite \f$B\f$ cannot see the negative curvature. BFGS reaches the minimum in both cases.

Neither method converges on the negative test cases: a noisy objective, \f$\sum_i \cosh x_i\f$ started from \f$700\f$ with a budget of \f$100\f$ iterations, and \f$\sum_i |x_i|\f$ with \f$n = 2, 4, 10\f$ under both line searches. The tests check that a converged run has \f$f - f^* \le 10^{-5}\f$, `decrement` \f$\le 10^{-6}\f$, `curvature_evaluations` \f$= 0\f$, and \f$\max_i |x_i - x^*_i|\f$ within \f$2 \times 10^{-3}\f$ (\f$5 \times 10^{-2}\f$ on Powell singular and \f$5 \times 10^{-3}\f$ on the quadratics). The details of the measurements are in `docs/numerics.md`.

## Example

The example minimizes the Rosenbrock function \f$f(x) = 100 (x_2 - x_1^2)^2 + (1 - x_1)^2\f$ from \f$x_0 = (-1.2, 1)\f$ with L-BFGS, after `Kokkos::initialize`.

```cpp
#include <laplace/optimizers/lbfgs.hpp>
#include <laplace/optimizers/minimize.hpp>

struct rosenbrock_objective {
  double value(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x) const {
    const double t1 = x(1) - x(0) * x(0);
    const double t2 = 1.0 - x(0);
    return 100.0 * t1 * t1 + t2 * t2;
  }
  double value_gradient(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x,
                        const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector& g) const {
    const double t1 = x(1) - x(0) * x(0);
    const double t2 = 1.0 - x(0);
    g(0) = -400.0 * x(0) * t1 - 2.0 * t2;
    g(1) = 200.0 * t1;
    return 100.0 * t1 * t1 + t2 * t2;
  }
};

bool rosenbrock_example() {
  laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector x0("x0", 2);
  x0(0) = -1.2;
  x0(1) = 1.0;
  rosenbrock_objective f;
  laplace::optimizers::lbfgs_optimizer<> opt;
  const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_report report = laplace::optimizers::minimize(opt, f, x0);
  return laplace::optimizers::optimizer_succeeded(report.status);
}
```

